From 3c6829c3fb6aa03b485851504c6276fd99d788cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:15:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/usb/hcd-dwc2: a transaction translator, so split
 transactions mean  something

The model stored HCSPLT and never read it.  A guest that set SPLTENA got
its transaction performed as if it were a direct one: every split
succeeded by accident, in one stage, with no ACK and no NYET -- which
are the two HCINT bits this model never set at all.

WHAT IS MODELLED.  One buffered result per channel, in DWC2Packet.  A
start-split performs the transaction against the downstream device --
usb_find_device() already recurses through hubs -- and leaves the answer
in p->packet with split_pending set, reporting the hub's ACK|CHHLTD and
no data.  The matching complete-split re-enters dwc2_handle_packet()
with send == false, which is the path an asynchronous completion already
takes, so the DMA write-back, the HCTSIZ/HCDMA bookkeeping and the
cleanup all happen exactly once in the existing tail.  A complete-split
with nothing buffered is answered NYET.

NYET and not NAK, and for a periodic endpoint the difference is the
whole protocol: NYET means "the translator has not finished, ask again"
while NAK is the DEVICE's own "nothing to report", which is terminal and
sends the guest back to a fresh start-split.  Answering NAK would make a
too-early complete-split indistinguishable from an idle endpoint.

A COMPLETE-SPLIT IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE DEVICE LOOKUP, and SET_ADDRESS
is the proof that it has to be.  On the wire a complete-split is
addressed to the hub; the device is not involved.  With the lookup first
-- and a failure returning silently, with no interrupt -- every
full-speed device behind a high-speed hub wedged at its new address:

	usb_set_addr dev 4
	usb_dwc2_enable_chan ch 0 dev 0x0 ep 0
	no device at addr 0 ep 0 hcsplt 8001c102

qemu applies SET_ADDRESS at the SETUP stage, so the device moves to
address 4 during that stage's START-split and the matching
COMPLETE-split, still correctly addressed to 0, finds nothing.  The
guest waited out twenty seconds and reported `completion timeout'.

AND THE CTRL/BULK NAK AUTO-RETRY IS NOT FOR A SPLIT CHANNEL.  It leaves
the channel enabled and sets needs_service, so dwc2_work_bh re-issues
the transaction every 250us -- right when the model is the only one
retrying, wrong when the guest's split state machine is.  On a split
channel both ran, the work queue re-running the same transaction behind
the guest's back.

WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, stated because the shape of the model bounds what
it can ever show.  There is no per-microframe scheduling and no
XACTPOS: a real translator buffers one transaction per periodic
endpoint per microframe, and this does not pretend to, because the
model has no usable microframe clock to pretend with --
dwc2_frame_boundary() divides (now - sof_time) by s->fi, which is
USB_FRMINTVL - 1 = 11999, a BIT-TIME count dividing NANOSECONDS, so
FRNUM jumps by about 83 per 1 ms tick and never by one.

WHAT IT BUYS, measured with a mutation test against a guest kernel
(Inferno on -M raspi3b) and not asserted:

  mutant                              split cfg   plain cfg
  ----------------------------------  ---------   ---------
  none                                ok 49       ok 52
  stale interrupt accumulator (RACE)  ok 49       ok 52
  exact-equality split phase test     FAIL        ok 52

The third is the real defect that stopped every full- and low-speed
device enumerating behind a hub on Broadcom silicon, and only the split
configuration notices.  The second is a race, and a model that runs the
whole transfer synchronously inside the CHEN store has no race to hit --
so this catches protocol defects and not timing ones, which is worth
knowing before anyone trusts it.

With it in, the guest reproduces the silicon failure exactly:

	usbd: ep2.0: port 2: full ep4.0 vid 0627 did 0001 class 0
	usbd: ep4.0: boot mouse on ep4.1
	usbdwc: ep4.1 INTR-IN NAK 1: hcint 00000012 COMPLETE-split

-- a periodic start-split answered ACK and the complete-split answered
NAK, which is what a real Pi 3B prints, where before the translator the
START-split was answered NAK because the model never raised ACK.

vmstate version 2 for split_pending, minimum left at 1: a version 1
stream leaves it false, which is the right default.
---
 hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.c | 138 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.h |  17 ++++++
 2 files changed, 154 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.c b/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.c
index 3d95ed5..02162c1 100644
--- a/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.c
+++ b/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.c
@@ -320,6 +320,46 @@ babble:
         return;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * THE START-SPLIT ENDS HERE, with the answer kept and not delivered.
+     *
+     * USB 2.0 11.17: a start-split hands the transaction to the hub's
+     * translator, which performs it on the downstream bus; the host then
+     * issues complete-splits to collect the result.  So the transaction
+     * has just been performed against the downstream device -- the
+     * lookup already recurses through hubs -- and what the guest must
+     * see now is the hub's acknowledgement, not the device's answer.
+     *
+     * p->packet is left INITIALISED and uncleaned on purpose: it is the
+     * buffer.  The matching complete-split re-enters this function with
+     * send == false, which is the same path an asynchronous completion
+     * takes, and the tail below then does the DMA write-back, the
+     * hctsiz/hcdma bookkeeping and the cleanup exactly once.
+     *
+     * An OUT start-split carries the data, and it has already gone: the
+     * dma_memory_read and usb_handle_packet above did it.  The
+     * complete-split then collects only the handshake, which is what the
+     * specification says it does.
+     */
+    if ((s->hreg1[index + 1] & HCSPLT_SPLTENA) &&
+        !(s->hreg1[index + 1] & HCSPLT_COMPSPLT)) {
+        p->devadr = devadr;
+        p->epnum = epnum;
+        p->epdir = epdir;
+        p->mps = mps;
+        p->pid = pid;
+        p->index = index;
+        p->pcnt = pcnt;
+        p->len = tlen;
+        p->split_pending = true;
+        p->needs_service = false;
+        hcchar &= ~HCCHAR_CHENA;
+        s->hreg1[index] = hcchar;
+        s->hreg1[index + 2] |= HCINTMSK_ACK | HCINTMSK_CHHLTD;
+        dwc2_update_hc_irq(s, index);
+        return;
+    }
+
     if (p->packet.status == USB_RET_SUCCESS) {
         if (actual > tlen) {
             p->packet.status = USB_RET_BABBLE;
@@ -357,11 +397,30 @@ babble:
     } else {
         intr |= pintr[stsidx];
         if (p->packet.status == USB_RET_NAK &&
+            !(s->hreg1[index + 1] & HCSPLT_SPLTENA) &&
             (eptype == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_CONTROL ||
              eptype == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_BULK)) {
             /*
              * for ctrl/bulk, automatically retry on NAK,
              * but send the interrupt anyway
+             *
+             * NOT FOR A SPLIT CHANNEL.  This retry leaves the channel
+             * enabled and sets needs_service, so dwc2_work_bh re-issues
+             * the transaction every 250us -- which is the right thing
+             * when the model is the only one retrying, and the wrong
+             * thing when the guest's own split state machine is.  On a
+             * split channel both run: the complete-split has already
+             * halted the channel and the guest is about to re-start the
+             * start-split, while the work queue re-runs the SAME
+             * transaction against the device behind its back.
+             *
+             * Measured: with this ungated, no full-speed device behind
+             * a high-speed hub got past its first transfer at its new
+             * address -- `ep4.0 completion timeout channel 0' and then
+             * `error intr 00000082' (Xacterr|Chhltd), twenty seconds of
+             * nothing.  A NAK on a split channel is the device's answer
+             * and belongs to the guest, which re-starts the start-split
+             * on it exactly as Linux's dwc2 does.
              */
             intr &= ~HCINTMSK_RESERVED14_31;
             s->hreg1[index + 2] |= intr;
@@ -631,6 +690,76 @@ static void dwc2_enable_chan(DWC2State *s,  uint32_t index)
     dev = dwc2_find_device(s, devadr);
 
     trace_usb_dwc2_enable_chan(index >> 3, dev, &p->packet, epnum);
+
+    /*
+     * A COMPLETE-SPLIT IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE DEVICE IS LOOKED UP, AND
+     * SET_ADDRESS IS THE PROOF THAT IT HAS TO BE.
+     *
+     * On the wire a complete-split is addressed to the HUB, not to the
+     * device: the translator already holds the answer and the device is
+     * not involved.  This model had it the other way round -- the
+     * device lookup came first and a failure returned silently, with no
+     * interrupt -- and SET_ADDRESS then wedged every full-speed device
+     * behind a high-speed hub.
+     *
+     * The sequence, traced:
+     *
+     *   usb_set_addr dev 4
+     *   usb_dwc2_enable_chan ch 0 dev 0x0 ep 0
+     *   TEMPDBG: no device at addr 0 ep 0 hcsplt 8001c102
+     *
+     * qemu applies SET_ADDRESS at the SETUP stage, so the device moves
+     * to address 4 during that stage's START-split; the guest then
+     * issues the matching COMPLETE-split still addressed to 0 -- which
+     * is correct, a control transfer does not change address
+     * mid-transfer -- and nothing answers to 0 any more.  The channel
+     * stayed enabled with no interrupt, the guest waited out
+     * chanwait's twenty seconds and reported
+     *
+     *   usbdwc: ep4.0 completion timeout channel 0
+     *   usbdwc: ep4.0 error intr 00000082
+     *
+     * dwc2_handle_packet() with send == false touches neither dev nor
+     * ep -- it is the same path an asynchronous completion takes -- so
+     * passing them through is harmless and the NULL is never
+     * dereferenced.
+     */
+    if (s->hreg1[index + 1] & HCSPLT_SPLTENA) {
+        if (s->hreg1[index + 1] & HCSPLT_COMPSPLT) {
+            if (!p->split_pending) {
+                /*
+                 * NYET, not NAK, and the difference is load-bearing for
+                 * a periodic endpoint: NYET means "the translator has
+                 * not finished, ask again" while NAK is the DEVICE's
+                 * own "nothing to report", which is terminal and sends
+                 * the guest back to a fresh start-split.  Answering NAK
+                 * here would make a too-early complete-split
+                 * indistinguishable from an idle endpoint.
+                 */
+                hcchar &= ~HCCHAR_CHENA;
+                s->hreg1[index] = hcchar;
+                s->hreg1[index + 2] |= HCINTMSK_NYET | HCINTMSK_CHHLTD;
+                dwc2_update_hc_irq(s, index);
+                return;
+            }
+            p->split_pending = false;
+            dwc2_handle_packet(s, devadr, dev, NULL, index, false);
+            qemu_bh_schedule(s->async_bh);
+            return;
+        }
+        /*
+         * A fresh start-split abandons whatever the last one left.
+         * dwc2 re-starts the start-split on a NAK or an error rather
+         * than retrying the complete-split, so an answer that was never
+         * collected is stale and must not be handed to the NEXT
+         * transaction's complete-split.
+         */
+        if (p->split_pending) {
+            p->split_pending = false;
+            usb_packet_cleanup(&p->packet);
+        }
+    }
+
     if (dev == NULL) {
         return;
     }
@@ -1330,6 +1459,7 @@ static void dwc2_reset_enter(Object *obj, ResetType type)
 
     for (i = 0; i < DWC2_NB_CHAN; i++) {
         s->packet[i].needs_service = false;
+        s->packet[i].split_pending = false;
     }
 }
 
@@ -1417,7 +1547,12 @@ static void dwc2_init(Object *obj)
 
 static const VMStateDescription vmstate_dwc2_state_packet = {
     .name = "dwc2/packet",
-    .version_id = 1,
+    /*
+     * 2: split_pending.  A stream from version 1 leaves it false, which
+     * is the right default -- no split in progress -- so the minimum is
+     * left at 1 and an older stream still loads.
+     */
+    .version_id = 2,
     .minimum_version_id = 1,
     .fields = (const VMStateField[]) {
         VMSTATE_UINT32(devadr, DWC2Packet),
@@ -1431,6 +1566,7 @@ static const VMStateDescription vmstate_dwc2_state_packet = {
         VMSTATE_INT32(async, DWC2Packet),
         VMSTATE_BOOL(small, DWC2Packet),
         VMSTATE_BOOL(needs_service, DWC2Packet),
+        VMSTATE_BOOL(split_pending, DWC2Packet),
         VMSTATE_END_OF_LIST()
     },
 };
diff --git a/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.h b/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.h
index 2d5a569..3631b05 100644
--- a/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.h
+++ b/hw/usb/hcd-dwc2.h
@@ -55,6 +55,23 @@ struct DWC2Packet {
     int32_t async;
     bool small;
     bool needs_service;
+    /*
+     * The transaction translator, all of it: one buffered result per
+     * channel.  A start-split runs the transaction against the
+     * downstream device and leaves the answer HERE, in p->packet, with
+     * this flag set; the matching complete-split hands that answer to
+     * the guest.  A complete-split with the flag clear is answered
+     * NYET, which is what "the translator has not finished" means.
+     *
+     * One per channel and not one per (hub, port, endpoint) because a
+     * channel is what the guest's split state machine owns: dwc2 holds
+     * the channel from the start-split to the complete-split and does
+     * not reuse it in between.  A real translator buffers per periodic
+     * endpoint per microframe; this does not pretend to, and the model
+     * has no usable microframe clock to pretend with -- see the note
+     * about s->fi in dwc2_frame_boundary().
+     */
+    bool split_pending;
 };
 
 struct DWC2State {
